 ( (a - b - b)/ (a - b) ) = r (a - 2b)/(a - b)\n\nBut this seems a bit messy. Maybe I made a mistake in the algebra. Let me check again.\n\nStarting from:\n\n- x + r = (-b/a)x + (b/a)r + r\n\nSubtract r from both sides:\n\n- x = (-b/a)x + (b/a)r\n\nThen add (b/a)x to both sides:\n\n- x + (b/a)x = (b/a) r\n\nFactor x:\n\nx( -1 + b/a ) = (b/a) r\n\nMultiply both sides by a to eliminate denominators:\n\nx( -a + b ) = b r\n\nSo x = (b r)/(b - a )\n\nAh, right, so x_N = (b r)/(b - a ) = - (b r)/(a - b )\n\nThen y_N = -x_N + r = (b r)/(a - b ) + r = r ( b/(a - b ) + 1 ) = r ( (b + a - b ) / (a - b ) ) = (a r)/(a - b )\n\nSo coordinates of N are ( - (b r)/(a - b ), (a r)/(a - b ) )\n\nAlternatively, since denominators can be written as (a - b), we can factor out:\n\nN = ( (b r)/(b - a ), (a r)/(a - b ) )\n\nBut maybe there's a better way to represent this. Alternatively, since a and b are positive, but depending on whether a > b or b > a, the signs might differ. However, since the triangle is right-angled, a and b can be any positive numbers. Perhaps we can assume without loss of generality that a \u2265 b, but maybe not. Alternatively, maybe using coordinate-free methods or vector approaches could help.\n\nAlternatively, maybe assigning specific values to a and b to simplify calculations. For example, let me choose a = 3, b = 4, which is a classic right triangle. Then hypotenuse c = 5. Let's see if this helps.\n\nWait, but if I take specific values, I can compute numerically and perhaps spot a pattern or even get the angle measure.\n\nLet's try that. Let\u2019s take a = 3, b = 4, c = 5. Then the inradius r = (3 + 4 - 5)/2 = (2)/2 = 1. So r = 1.\n\nTherefore, the incenter is at (1,1). The points P and Q are (1,0) and (0,1), so PQ is the line connecting (1,0) and (0,1), which is y = -x + 1.\n\nThe hypotenuse AB is from (3,0) to (0,4). The line parallel to AB passing through the incenter (1,1) would have the same slope as AB. The slope of AB is (4 - 0)/(0 - 3) = -4/3. So the