nsitions force the game into a certain structure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the key is to look for primes that have only one outgoing edge or no outgoing edges, as these can be terminal states. For example, if a prime ends with a digit that has no primes starting with that digit, then the next player cannot move. Let's check if any primes end with a digit that has no primes starting with that digit.\n\nLooking at the last digits:\n\nPrimes can end with 1, 2, 3, 5, 7, 9 (since primes greater than 5 can't end with 0, 2, 4, 5, 6, 8 except for 2 and 5 themselves).\n\nNow, which digits have primes starting with them:\n\nStarting digits are 1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9.\n\nWait, primes starting with 1: yes, 11,13,17,19.\n\nStarting with 2: 2,23,29.\n\nStarting with 3: 3,31,37.\n\nStarting with 4:41,43,47.\n\nStarting with 5:5,53,59.\n\nStarting with 6:61,67.\n\nStarting with 7:7,71,73,79.\n\nStarting with 8:83,89.\n\nStarting with 9:97.\n\nSo all digits from 1 to 9 except for 0 have primes starting with them. But the last digits can be 1,2,3,5,7,9. So, the possible transitions are from last digits 1,2,3,5,7,9 to starting digits 1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9. Wait, but the transition must be from last digit to starting digit. So, for example, a prime ending with 1 can transition to any prime starting with 1. Similarly, ending with 2 transitions to primes starting with 2, etc.\n\nSo, each last digit corresponds to starting digits for the next prime. Therefore, every possible last digit (1,2,3,5,7,9) has primes starting with those digits, except maybe some digits?\n\nWait, let's check each last digit:\n\nLast digit 1: primes starting with 1 are 11,13,17,19.\n\nLast digit 2: primes starting with 2 are 2,23,29.\n\nLast digit 3: primes starting with 3 are 3,31,37.\n\nLast digit 5: primes starting with 5 are 5,53,59.\n\nLast digit 7: primes starting with 7 are 7,71,73,79.\n\nLast digit 9: primes starting with 9 is 97.\n\nSo, for each last digit (1,2,3,5,7,9), there exists at least one prime starting with that digit. So, in theory, no immediate dead-ends unless all primes starting with that digit have already been used.\n\nTherefore, the terminal positions occur when all primes starting with the required digit have already been used. Therefore, the game ends when a player cannot choose an unused prime starting with the required digit.\n\nTherefore, the key is to model this as a directed graph with nodes as primes and edges as allowed transitions, and then analyze the game as a DAG (directed acyclic graph) game, even though there might be cycles.\n\nHowever, since primes cannot be repeated, the game is actually played on the graph where each node can be visited at most once. So, it's equivalent to a game on a directed graph with the \"no repeating nodes\" rule, which is known as the vertex-offering game. The analysis of such games can be complex, but perhaps certain properties can be leveraged.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can model this as an impartial game where each position is a prime, and moves correspond to following an edge to another prime. Since the game is impartial (both players have the same moves available from a position), we can try to assign Grundy numbers to each prime. The Grundy number of a node is the mex (minimum excludant) of the Grundy numbers of its neighbors. If a position has a Grundy number of 0, it's a losing position; otherwise, it's a winning position.\n\nHowever, computing Grundy numbers for all 25 primes might be tedious, but perhaps manageable. Let me try to outline the steps.\n\nFirst, note that the game is a finite impartial game under the normal play condition (last player to move wins). Therefore, each position has a Grundy number. The Grundy number for a node with no outgoing edges is 0. For other nodes, it's the mex of the Grundy numbers of its neighbors.\n\nHowever, since the game starts with any prime, the first player can choose any prime as the starting node. Therefore, if there exists a prime with a Grundy number of 0, the