ring for a small angular displacement \u03b8.\n\n2. Compute the resulting force from each spring.\n\n3. Find the torque from each spring about axis O.\n\n4. Sum the torques to get the total restoring torque, which is -\u03ba\u03b8, defining \u03ba.\n\n5. Compute the moment of inertia I for the mass.\n\n6. Use T = 2\u03c0\u221a(I/\u03ba).\n\nAssuming the mass is at the end of the rod, which is a distance L from O, and the springs are at L/3 and 2L/3. Then, the displacements x1 = (L/3)\u03b8 and x2 = (2L/3)\u03b8. Forces F1 = k1x1 and F2 = k2x2. Torques \u03c41 = F1*(L/3) = k1*(L/3)^2*\u03b8, \u03c42 = F2*(2L/3) = k2*(2L/3)^2*\u03b8. Total torque \u03c4 = -(k1*(L/3)^2 + k2*(2L/3)^2)*\u03b8. Therefore, \u03ba = (k1*(L^2)/9 + 4k2*(L^2)/9) = (k1 + 4k2)L\u00b2/9. I = m*L\u00b2. Then, T = 2\u03c0\u221a(I/\u03ba) = 2\u03c0\u221a(m*L\u00b2 / [(k1 + 4k2)L\u00b2/9]) ) = 2\u03c0\u221a(9m/(k1 +4k2)). All correct.\n\nTherefore, the period is 2\u03c0\u221a(9m/(k1 +4k2)) which simplifies to 6\u03c0\u221a(m/(k1 +4k2)). Wait, because \u221a(9m) = 3\u221am. So, 2\u03c0*3\u221a(m/(k1 +4k2)) = 6\u03c0\u221a(m/(k1 +4k2)). Let's verify:\n\n\u221a(9m/(k1 +4k2)) = \u221a9 * \u221a(m/(k1 +4k2)) = 3\u221a(m/(k1 +4k2)). Then, 2\u03c0 multiplied by that is 6\u03c0\u221a(m/(k1 +4k2)). But in numbers:\n\nCompute sqrt(m/(k1 +4k2)):\n\nm = 1.6 kg\n\nk1 +4k2 = 40 N/m\n\nsqrt(1.6 / 40) = sqrt(0.04) = 0.2\n\nThen, 6\u03c0*0.2 = 1.2\u03c0 \u2248 3.7699 seconds. Same result.\n\nAlternatively, 6\u03c0*0.2 is indeed 1.2\u03c0. So, 1.2\u03c0 seconds. So, exact answer is 6\u03c0/5 seconds, since 1.2 = 6/5. Because 6/5 = 1.2. So, T = (6\u03c0/5) seconds \u2248 3.7699 seconds.\n\nThus, the period is 6\u03c0/5 seconds.\n\nBut let me check if there's an alternative interpretation. Suppose the axis O is not at the end of the rod but somewhere else. For example, if the rod is divided into three equal parts by the springs, then perhaps the axis O is at one of the division points. Let's suppose O is at the first sp