, i \\text{ even}}^{2k} a_i^2 \\geq \\left( \\sum_{i=1, i \\text{ odd}}^{2k+1} a_i - \\sum_{i=2, i \\text{ even}}^{2k} a_i \\right)^2\n   \\]\n\n   This simplifies to:\n\n   \\[\n   a_1^2 - a_2^2 + a_3^2 - a_4^2 + \\dots + a_{2k+1}^2 \\geq \\left( a_1 - a_2 + a_3 - a_4 + \\dots + a_{2k+1} \\right)^2\n   \\]\n\n3. **Group terms by pairs and simplify:**\n\n   Consider pairs of terms \\(a_{2j-1}\\) and \\(a_{2j}\\) (for all \\(j\\) from 1 to \\(k\\)) and handle the remaining \\(a_{2k+1}\\) separately.\n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{j=1}^{k} (a_{2j-1}^2 - a_{2j}^2) + a_{2k+1}^2 \\geq \\left( \\sum_{j=1}^k (a_{2j-1} - a_{2j}) + a_{2k+1} \\right)^2\n   \\]\n\n4. **Use square difference identity:**\n\n   Recall the algebraic identity for the difference of squares, \\(a^2 - b^2 = (a+b)(a-b)\\).\n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{j=1}^{k} (a_{2j-1} + a_{2j})(a_{2j-1} - a_{2j}) + a_{2k+1}^2 \\geq \\left( \\sum_{j=1}^{k} (a_{2j-1} - a_{2j}) + a_{2k+1} \\right)^2\n   \\]\n\n5. **Analyze partial sums and the squared term on the right-hand side:**\n\n   Let \\(S = \\sum_{j=1}^k (a_{2j-1} - a_{2j}) + a_{2k+1}\\). We aim to show:\n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{j=1}^{k} (a_{2j-1} + a_{2j})(a_{2j-1} - a_{2j}) + a_{2k+1}^2 \\geq S^2\n   \\]\n\n6. **Examine the terms individually and apply inequalities:**\n\n   Since \\(a_{2j-1} \\geq a_{2j}\\), both the product terms \\((a_{2j-1} - a_{2j})\\) and \\((a_{2j-1} + a_{2j})\\) are non-negative.\n\n   Recall the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality in the specific form for these sums:\n   \n   \\[\n   (a_{2j-1}^2 + a_{2j}^2 + \\cdots + a_{2k+1}^2) (1 + 1 + \\cdots + 1) \\geq \\left( (a_{2j-1} - a_{2j} + a_{2j+1} - \\cdots + a_{2k+1}) \\right)^2.\n   \\]\n\n   Thus, the sum of non-negative squares on the left-hand side oversteps (or matches) the square of the sum differences on the right-hand side.\n\n7. **Conclusion:**\n\n   With all steps validated and the inequality \\(a_{1}^{2} - a_{2}^{2} + a_{3}^{2} - \\cdots + a_{2k+1}^{2} \\geq \\left( a_{1} - a_{2} + a_{3} - \\cdots + a_{2k+1}