 2(x\u00b2 +4x +4)=2(x +2)^2. So, root at x=-2. Correct.\n\nTherefore, the roots are indeed 0 and -2.\n\nHence, the answer should be 0 and -2. So, separated by semicolons: 0; -2.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe roots can be \\boxed{0} and \\boxed{-2}. So, written as: \\boxed{0}; \\boxed{-2}", "deepseek_solution": "Let the original quadratic trinomial be \\( ax^2 + bx + c \\). When the second coefficient \\( b \\) and the constant term \\( c \\) are switched, the new trinomial becomes \\( ax^2 + cx + b \\). Adding these two trinomials together results in:\n\n\\[\n(ax^2 + bx + c) + (ax^2 + cx + b) = 2ax^2 + (b + c)x + (b + c)\n\\]\n\nThis resulting trinomial \\( 2ax^2 + (b + c)x + (b + c) \\) must have a single root, meaning its discriminant must be zero. The discriminant \\( D \\) of this quadratic is:\n\n\\[\nD = (b + c)^2 - 4 \\cdot 2a \\cdot (b + c)\n\\]\n\nSetting the discriminant to zero gives:\n\n\\[\n(b + c)^2 - 8a(b + c) = 0\n\\]\n\nFactoring out \\( (b + c) \\):\n\n\\[\n(b + c)(b + c - 8a) = 0\n\\]\n\nThis results in two cases:\n\n1. \\( b + c = 0 \\)\n2. \\( b + c = 8a \\)\n\nFor the first case (\\( b + c = 0 \\)), the resulting trinomial simplifies to \\( 2ax^2 \\), which has a double root at \\( x = 0 \\).\n\nFor the second case (\\( b + c = 8a \\)), the resulting trinomial becomes \\( 2ax^2 + 8ax + 8a \\), which factors to \\( 2a(x + 2)^2 \\), giving a double root at \\( x = -2 \\).\n\nThus, the possible roots of the resulting trinomial are \\( 0 \\) and \\( -2 \\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}; \\boxed{-2}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\nGiven a quadratic trinomial of the form \\( ax^2 + bx + c \\), we perform the following steps to determine the possible value(s) of the unique root, given some specific transformations and conditions:\n\n1. Start with the quadratic trinomial: \n   \\[\n   ax^2 + bx + c\n   \\]\n2. Swap the second coefficient \\( b \\) (the linear term) and the constant \\( c \\):\n   \\[\n   ax^2 + cx + b\n   \\]\n3. Add the transformed trinomial to 