d have two on the main diagonal. Wait, no. (1,1) is on the main diagonal, (2,3) is not, (3,2) is on the anti-diagonal. So each is on a different diagonal. However, the center cell (2,2) is on both diagonals. So if a digit is placed in three different cells, avoiding diagonals, but in a 3x3 grid, it's impossible to have three cells that are all not on any diagonal. Because the diagonals cover five cells: four corners and the center. The edge cells (like top-center, center-left) are not on diagonals. There are four edge cells. So to place three instances of a digit in edge cells, but each in different rows and columns. However, there are only four edge cells, and placing three in different rows and columns would require three edge cells, each in distinct rows and columns. The edge cells are:\n\n- Top-center: row 1, column 2\n\n- Center-left: row 2, column 1\n\n- Center-right: row 2, column 3\n\n- Bottom-center: row 3, column 2\n\nThese four cells. To pick three that are in distinct rows and columns. For example, top-center (1,2), center-left (2,1), bottom-center (3,2). But column 2 has two cells (top-center and bottom-center). So that's invalid. Alternatively, top-center (1,2), center-right (2,3), bottom-center (3,2). Again, column 2 has two. Another combination: top-center (1,2), center-left (2,1), center-right (2,3). But row 2 has two cells. Not allowed. Therefore, it's impossible to place three instances of a digit in the edge cells without repeating a row or column. Hence, the maximum number of times a digit can appear is two: once in a corner and once in an edge cell not conflicting with diagonals, or twice in edge cells in different rows and columns.\n\nTherefore, each digit can appear at most two times in the grid. Since there are four digits, the maximum total number of cells we can fill without violating the constraints is 4*2=8. However, the grid has nine cells. Therefore, it's impossible to fill all nine cells with four digits, each appearing at most twice,