ed four (due to it covering two diagonally opposite cells), it is impossible to place this tile in such a way as to cover exactly four leftover cells of distinct colors.\n   \n   Therefore, it is not possible to cover an \\(8 \\times 8\\) grid with fifteen \\(4 \\times 1\\) tiles and one \\(2 \\times 2\\) tile.\n\n\\(\\boxed{\\text{Proof Complete}}\\)", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let \\( p \\) and \\( q \\) be distinct prime numbers. Determine the integer solutions \\((a, b)\\) of\n\n\\[ \\frac{1}{a} + \\frac{1}{b} = \\frac{1}{pq} \\]", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let me try to figure out the integer solutions (a, b) for the equation 1/a + 1/b = 1/(pq), where p and q are distinct primes. Hmm, so I need to find all pairs of integers a and b such that when you add their reciprocals, you get 1/(pq). \n\nFirst, maybe I can rewrite the equation to make it easier to handle. If I combine the fractions on the left side, I get (b + a)/(ab) = 1/(pq). Then, cross-multiplying both sides should eliminate the denominators. That would give me pq(b + a) = ab. Let me check that again: multiplying both sides by abpq, yeah, that cancels out the denominators correctly. So, pq(a + b) = ab. \n\nOkay, so now the equation is ab - pq(a + b) = 0. Maybe I can rearrange terms to factor this somehow. Let's see, if I add (pq)^2 to both sides, maybe that helps. Let's try:\n\nab - pq(a + b) + (pq)^2 = (pq)^2.\n\nThen, the left side can be factored as (a - pq)(b - pq) = (pq)^2. Wait, let me verify that. Let's expand (a - pq)(b - pq):\n\nab - pq a - pq b + (pq)^2. Yeah, exactly! So that's equal to the left side after adding (pq)^2. So, indeed, (a - pq)(b - pq) = (pq)^2.\n\nSo now, the problem reduces to finding all pairs of integers (a, b) such that (a - pq) and (b - pq) are integer divisors of (pq)^2. Since p and q are distinct primes, (pq)^2 has a specific set of divisors. The divisors of (pq)^2 are 1, p, q, p^2, q^2, pq, p^2