). Thus, the solutions are \\(\\boxed{\\left( pq + d, \\ pq + \\frac{(pq)^2}{d} \\right)}\\) for each divisor \\(d\\) of \\((pq)^2\\) other than \\(-pq\\).", "deepseek_solution": "To determine the integer solutions \\((a, b)\\) of the equation\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{a} + \\frac{1}{b} = \\frac{1}{pq}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) are distinct prime numbers, we start by rewriting the equation. Combining the fractions on the left side, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{a + b}{ab} = \\frac{1}{pq}\n\\]\n\nCross-multiplying both sides, we obtain:\n\n\\[\npq(a + b) = ab\n\\]\n\nRearranging terms, we have:\n\n\\[\nab - pq(a + b) = 0\n\\]\n\nAdding \\((pq)^2\\) to both sides allows us to factor the left-hand side:\n\n\\[\nab - pq(a + b) + (pq)^2 = (pq)^2\n\\]\n\nThis can be factored as:\n\n\\[\n(a - pq)(b - pq) = (pq)^2\n\\]\n\nThus, the problem reduces to finding all pairs of integers \\((a - pq)\\) and \\((b - pq)\\) that multiply to \\((pq)^2\\). Let \\(d\\) be a divisor of \\((pq)^2\\). Then, we can write:\n\n\\[\na - pq = d \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b - pq = \\frac{(pq)^2}{d}\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(a\\) and \\(b\\), we get:\n\n\\[\na = pq + d \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b = pq + \\frac{(pq)^2}{d}\n\\]\n\nWe must exclude the divisor \\(d = -pq\\) because it would lead to \\(a\\) or \\(b\\) being zero, which is invalid. Therefore, the integer solutions \\((a, b)\\) are given by all pairs:\n\n\\[\n\\left( pq + d, \\ pq + \\frac{(pq)^2}{d} \\right)\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(d\\) is any divisor of \\((pq)^2\\) except \\(d = -pq\\).\n\n### Final Answer\nThe integer solutions are all pairs \\(\\left( pq + d, \\ pq + \\frac{(pq)^2}{d} \\right)\\) where \\(d\\) is any divisor of \\((pq)^2\\) except \\(d = -pq\\). Thus, the solutions are \\(\\boxed{\\left( pq + d, \\ pq + \\frac{(pq)^2}{d} \\right)}\\) for each divisor \\(d\\) of \\((pq)^2\\) other than \\(-pq\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\nTo find the integer solutions \\((a, b)\\) to the equation \n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{a} + \\frac{1}{b} = \\frac{1}{pq}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) are distinct prime numbers, let's convert this equation into a polynomial equation in integers.\n\n1. Starting from the given equation:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{a} + \\frac{1}{b} = \\frac{1}{pq}\n   \\]\n\n2. Take the common denominator on the left-hand side:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{b + a}{ab} = \\frac{1}{pq}\n   \\]\n\n3. Cross-multiplying to clear the fractions:\n   \\[\n   (b + a) \\cdot pq = ab\n   \\]\n\n4. Rearrange the equation to isolate terms involving \\(a\\) and \\(b\\):\n   \\[\n   ab - pq(a + b) = 0\n   \\]\n\n5. Add \\(p^2q^2\\) to both sides to manipulate the equation into a factored form:\n   \\[\n   ab - pq(a + b) + p^2q^2 = p^2q^2\n   \\]\n\n6. The left-hand side can now be factored:\n   \\[\n   (a - pq)(b - pq) = p^2 q^2\n   \\]\n\nNext, we will find all possible pairs \\((a, b)\\) by considering all the divisor pairs of \\(p^2 q^2\\). The prime factorization of \\(p^2 q^2\\) gives that \\(p^2 q^2\\) has \\((2 + 1) \\times (2 + 1) = 9\\) factors.\n\nFor each factor pair \\((d, \\frac{p^2 q^2}{d})\\), we set:\n\\[\n(a - pq) = d \\quad \\text{and} \\quad (b - pq) = \\frac{p^2 q^2}{d}\n\\]\n\n7. Therefore:\n   \\[\n   a = d + pq\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   b = \\frac{p^2 q^2}{d} + pq\n   \\]\n\nEnumerating all divisor pairs \\((d, \\frac{p^2 q^2}{d})\\) of \\(p^2 q^2\\):\n\n- \\(d = 1\\):\n  \\[\n  a = 1 + pq, \\quad b = p^2 q^2 + pq\n  \\]\n- \\(d = p\\):\n  \\[\n  a = p + pq = p(q + 1), \\quad b = \\frac{p^2 q^2}{p} + pq = pq(q + 1)\n  \\]\n- \\(d = q\\):\n  \\[\n  a = q + pq = q(p + 1), \\quad b = \\frac{p^2 q^2}{q} + pq = pq(p + 1)\n  \\]\n- \\(d = p^2\\):\n  \\[\n  a = p^2 + pq = p(p + q), \\quad b = \\frac{p^2 q^2}{p^2} + pq = q^2 + pq\n  \\]\n- \\(d = q^2\\):\n  \\[\n  a = q^2 + pq = q^2 + pq, \\quad b = \\frac{p^2 q^2}{q^2} + pq = p^2 + pq\n  \\]\n- \\(d = pq\\):\n  \\[\n  a = pq + pq = 2pq, \\quad b = \\frac{p^2 q^2}{pq} + pq = pq + pq = 2pq\n  \\]\n- \\(d = p^2q\\):\n  \\[\n  a = p^2 q + pq = pq(p + 1), \\quad b = \\frac{p^2 q^2}{p^2 q} + pq = q + pq = q(p + 1)\n  \\]\n- \\(d = pq^2\\):\n  \\[\n  a = pq^2 + pq = pq(q + 1), \\quad b = \\frac{p^2 q^2}{pq^2} + pq = p + pq = p(q + 1)\n  \\]\n- \\(d = p^2q^2\\):\n  \\[\n  a = p^2 q^2 + pq, \\quad b = 1 + pq\n  \\]\n\nThus, summarizing all the solutions:\n\\[\n(a, b) : \\{(1 + pq, p^2 q^2 + pq), (p(q + 1), pq(q + 1)), (q(p + 1), pq(p + 1)), (2pq, 2pq), (p^2 q(p + q), q^2 + pq), (q^2 + pq, p^2 + pq), (pq(p + 1), q(p + 1)), (pq(q + 1), p(q + 1)), (p^2 q^2 + pq, 1 + pq)\\}\n\\]\n\nSo, the integer solutions \\((a, b)\\) are provided above. The number of solutions equals the number of divisor pairs of \\(p^2 q^2\\), which is 9. \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(1 + pq, p^2 q^2 + pq), (p(q + 1), pq(q + 1)), (q(p + 1), pq(p + 1)), (2pq, 2pq), (p^2 q(p + q), q^2 + pq), (q^2 + pq, p^2 + pq), (pq(p + 1), q(p + 1)), (pq(q + 1), p(q + 1)), (p^2 q^2 + pq, 1 + pq)}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "There is one black ball and one white ball in a bag. Each time a ball is taken from the bag, it is not returned, but instead, another black ball is added. What is the probability of drawing a black ball on the $n$-th draw?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so there's this problem with a bag that starts with one black ball and one white ball. Every time you draw a ball, you don't put it back, but instead, you add another black ball. The question is asking for the probability that the nth draw is a black ball. Hmm, let me try to figure this out step by step.\n\nFirst, let me understand the process. Initially, there are two balls: 1 black (B) and 1 white (W). When you draw a ball, you don't put it back, but you add a black one. So, after each draw, the total number of balls remains the same, right? Because you take one out and add another. So the number of balls is always 2, then 2 again, and so on. Wait, no, hold on. Wait, initially, there are 2 balls. If you draw one, you remove it, so there's 1 left, then