airs of cards $(a, b)$ and $(b, c)$:\n    \\[\n    \\text{Transforms to } (a, c)\n    \\]\n    The differences satisfy: \n    \\[\n    a - c = (a - b) + (b - c)\n    \\]\n\nFrom the operations above, it is clear that the difference between the numbers on the cards is crucial. We need to find the invariant quantity that moves through these operations.\n\n#### Investigating Invariants:\n\nTrying out sequences of operations, we generate some cards to observe patterns:\n1. Starting with $(5, 19)$:\n    \\[\n    (5, 19) \\to (6, 20) \\quad \\text{Operation 1}\n    \\]\n    Difference: \n    \\[\n    6 - 20 = -14\n    \\]\n\n2. Making numbers even for Operation 2:\n    \\[\n    (6, 20) \\to (3, 10) \\quad \\text{Operation 2}\n    \\]\n    Difference:\n    \\[\n    3 - 10 = -7\n    \\]\n\n3. From $(3, 10)$ back to $(6, 20)$:\n    \\[\n    (3, 10) \\to (20, 27) \\quad \\text{Operation 1} \\times 7,  \\text{(repeated application)}\n    \\]\n    Difference: \n    \\[\n    20 - 27 = -7\n    \\]\n\nCombining cards:\n    \\[\n    (6, 20), (20, 27) \\to (6, 27) \\quad \\text{Operation 3}\n    \\]\n    Difference: \n    \\[\n    6 - 27 = -21\n    \\]\n\nWe observe differences:\n\\[\n14, 7, 21\n\\]\n\n#### Invariant Property:\nThe differences in the numbers (modulo operation) always seem to be multiples of 7:\n\\[\na - b \\equiv 0 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\nStarting from 5 times modulo 7:\n    \\[\n    5 - 19 = -14 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{7}\n    \\]\n\nTo achieve another card like $(1, 1988)$:\n    \\[\n    1 - 1988 = -1987\n    \\]\nThis is:\n\\[\n1987 \\mod 7 \\neq 0\n\\]\nIt shares no divisibility with 7.\n\n### Conclusion:\nSince the difference of the desired card $(1, 1988)$ does not share factor properties (multiples of 7), transforming $(5, 19)$ to $(1, 1988)$ using given operations is impossible.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{No}}\n\\]\n", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}]